Hi Andrew,

Thanks for the KIP! I don=E2=80=99t have much experience as a Kafka =
operator, but this seems like a very sane proposal.

Questions & comments:

1. Do you think the ListGroupsResult.all() method is a bit of a =
potential =E2=80=98foot gun=E2=80=99? I can imagine cases where =
developers reach for that without understanding its potential of =
throwing errors. It could lead to cases where all() works in development =
but not in production.

2. Based on the LIST_GROUPS RPC, it appears that filtering is all =
performed client side, correct? (I know that=E2=80=99s not specific to =
this KIP, but just want to make sure I understand.)

3. For kafka-groups.sh --list, is =E2=80=98classic=E2=80=99 valid for =
--group-type? If so, should we allow users of kafka-groups.sh --list to =
provide multiple --group-type arguments?

4. In the last kafka-share-groups.sh --create example =
(=E2=80=9CConsumerGroup=E2=80=9D), the error simply states that =E2=80=9CG=
roup 'ConsumerGroup=E2=80=99 is not a share group.=E2=80=9D I=E2=80=99m =
assuming that=E2=80=99s the case where the user gets a failure when =
there=E2=80=99s already a group named =E2=80=9CConsumerGroup=E2=80=9D, =
right? If so, the error should be something like =E2=80=9CThere is =
already a (consumer) group named =E2=80=99ConsumerGroup=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D.=


5. In the last kafka-share-groups.sh --describe example, how hard is it =
to add the type of group that CG1 is, just for a bit of clarity for the =
user?

6. In the kafka-consumer-groups.sh section, it states "if that group =
exists but is not a consumer group, the command fails with a message =
indicating that the group type is incorrect, rather than the existing =
message that the group does not exist.=E2=80=9D That sounds like a =
change that could trip up some brittle scripts somewhere, but I don=E2=80=99=
t know if that=E2=80=99s a serious problem.

Thanks!

> On Jun 4, 2024, at 10:08=E2=80=AFAM, Andrew Schofield =
<andrew_schofield@live.com> wrote:
>=20
> Hi,
> I would like to start a discussion thread on KIP-1043: Administration =
of groups. This KIP enhances the command-line tools to make it easier to =
administer groups on clusters with a variety of types of groups.
>=20
> =
https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-1043%3A+Administrati=
on+of+groups
>=20
> Thanks.
> Andrew


